Sure! To see whether your **`parameterObject`** and **`variableDeclarationObject`** share the same set of declaration flags—taking into account things like optionality (`isOptional`), visibility modifiers (`private`, `protected`, etc.), async/abstract/static/read-only attributes—you can feed both objects into the helper function I’ve got available:

```json
{
  "name": "areDeclarationFlagsIdentical",
  "arguments": {
    "left": "<your_parameter_object_as_a_string>",
    "right": "<your_variable_declaration_object_as_a_string>"
  }
}
```

Replace `<...>` with the *JSON‑serialised* form of each declaration object you’re comparing. For example:

```json
{
  "name":"areDeclarationFlagsIdentical",
  "arguments":{
     "left":"{\"kind\":\"Parameter\",\"modifiers\":[\"public\", \"readonly\"],\"optional\":true}",
     "right":"{\"kind\":\"VariableStatement\",\"modifiers\":[\"const\", \"readonly\"]}"
  }
}
```

When you run this through the system, it will return something like:

| Result | Meaning |
|--------|---------|
| `true` | All relevant flags match (including allowed differences such as an “optional” flag on a parameter vs. none on a variable). |
| `false`| At least one significant flag differs (e.g., one is private while the other isn’t, or one is async but the other isn’t). |

If you’d like me to perform the comparison now, just paste the exact stringified versions of your two declaration objects here, and I’ll invoke the function for you.
